Gabriel Perez, a longtime White House teleprompter operator for President Donald Trump, is no longer employed by the federal government following allegations that he used privileged knowledge of presidential speeches to place bets on the online prediction market Kalshi. A White House official confirmed Perez's departure but did not specify whether he resigned or was dismissed. Earlier this month, Perez had been placed on unpaid administrative leave after reports surfaced alleging he profited from wagers based on the president's prepared remarks. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

According to investigators and previous media reports, Perez allegedly earned more than $100,000 by betting on whether Trump would use specific words or phrases during major public addresses, including the State of the Union. Because Perez worked directly with teleprompter scripts before the speeches were delivered, authorities are examining whether he possessed material non-public information that gave him an unfair advantage in prediction markets. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

The allegations have drawn significant attention because they involve a senior White House employee with direct access to confidential presidential speech preparations. Ethics experts say the case raises broader concerns about the use of insider government information in rapidly expanding prediction markets.

The investigation began after Kalshi's internal surveillance systems detected unusual betting patterns linked to Perez's account. The prediction market platform launched an internal review before referring its findings to the U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC), the federal regulator responsible for overseeing Kalshi's exchange. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

Kalshi's rules prohibit users from placing trades based on confidential information obtained through their employment or official duties. Company officials said the platform continuously monitors trading activity for suspicious patterns and reports potential violations to regulators when appropriate.

Federal investigators are examining whether Perez's access to presidential speech drafts constituted insider information under applicable market rules. Although prediction markets differ from traditional securities markets, regulators increasingly view misuse of confidential information as a significant enforcement issue.

The case has also intensified debate over how prediction markets should be regulated as they continue expanding into political events, elections, and public policy forecasts. Industry supporters argue these markets improve information discovery, while critics contend they create opportunities for unethical conduct when participants possess privileged information. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

CBS News has announced a significantly reshaped roster of correspondents and contributors for the upcoming 59th season of '60 Minutes,' marking one of the most substantial personnel changes in the program's nearly six-decade history. The announcement follows months of internal restructuring at CBS News and signals the beginning of a new editorial chapter for one of American television's most respected investigative news programs. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

Among the most notable additions is former 'CBS Evening News' anchor Norah O'Donnell, who will become a full-time correspondent after previously contributing occasional reports. She is joined by New York Times columnist Ross Douthat, author and filmmaker Sebastian Junger, former Vice News correspondent Gianna Toboni, and veteran British broadcaster Trevor Phillips, all of whom will contribute reporting during the new season. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

Longtime correspondents Lesley Stahl, Bill Whitaker and Jon Wertheim will remain central figures on the broadcast, providing continuity alongside the incoming journalists. Network executives described the expanded team as a blend of experienced investigative reporters, international correspondents, documentary filmmakers and opinion writers capable of delivering a broad range of stories.

The 59th season is expected to premiere in September, with producers indicating that multiple reports featuring the new team are already in production. Executives said the goal is to preserve the program's reputation for in-depth journalism while adapting to evolving audience expectations across broadcast, streaming and digital platforms. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

The incoming correspondents represent diverse backgrounds across journalism, documentary filmmaking and international reporting. Ross Douthat joins after a lengthy career as a columnist and author known for writing about politics, religion and culture. Although best known for opinion journalism, CBS executives praised his analytical approach and ability to explore complex public policy issues through long-form reporting. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

Norah O'Donnell's expanded role builds upon decades of experience covering national politics, presidential administrations, foreign affairs and major breaking news events. As one of CBS News' most recognizable journalists, her move into a permanent '60 Minutes' position reflects the network's emphasis on experienced investigative reporting.

Sebastian Junger brings extensive documentary and conflict-reporting experience, having covered wars, humanitarian crises and military operations around the world. Gianna Toboni has earned recognition for investigative documentaries examining criminal justice, international conflict and social issues, while Trevor Phillips contributes decades of experience covering global political developments and international affairs. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}

CBS executives said the combination of veteran broadcasters and newer voices is intended to broaden the program's storytelling capabilities while maintaining its commitment to deeply reported journalism. The network believes the expanded roster will allow '60 Minutes' to pursue investigations spanning domestic politics, international affairs, technology, culture and business.

The staffing announcement follows a period of considerable change within CBS News. Earlier this year, executive leadership introduced a broad restructuring that resulted in the departure of several longtime correspondents and producers. Veteran journalists Scott Pelley, Sharyn Alfonsi, Cecilia Vega and Anderson Cooper all exited the program, while longtime executive producer Tanya Simon was replaced by Nick Bilton as part of the broader editorial transition. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}

The changes occurred after CBS News underwent wider organisational restructuring under new editorial leadership. Executives described the overhaul as an effort to modernise the news division while maintaining the journalistic standards that have defined '60 Minutes' since its debut in 1968.

The departures generated significant discussion throughout the journalism industry, with observers debating how the changes might influence the editorial direction of the iconic broadcast. Some former staff members publicly expressed concerns about editorial independence, while CBS leadership has maintained that the restructuring is designed to strengthen investigative reporting and position the programme for long-term success.

Nick Bilton, now serving as executive producer, has stated that the programme will continue prioritising independent journalism, long-form storytelling and accountability reporting. He has emphasised attracting journalists capable of reaching audiences across traditional television as well as emerging digital platforms. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}

The U.S. Department of Defense has introduced a new category within its Defense Casualty Analysis System to account for American service members killed or wounded during renewed military operations against Iran following the official conclusion of Operation Epic Fury. Pentagon officials said the change reflects a distinct operational phase that began after hostilities resumed in July, rather than an effort to reduce or conceal the total number of casualties. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

The new classification, labelled 'Overseas Operations,' includes casualties sustained after renewed fighting broke out following the collapse of a temporary ceasefire. Previously, these losses had been counted alongside casualties from Operation Epic Fury, the large-scale military campaign launched earlier in the year. Pentagon officials argued that separating the two phases provides a more accurate representation of distinct military operations.

The update has nevertheless attracted attention because casualty totals associated with Operation Epic Fury declined after the new category was introduced, even though the overall number of U.S. military personnel killed or wounded did not change. Officials stressed that the total casualty figures remain publicly available across both reporting categories.

The revised reporting system has become a focus of congressional scrutiny and public discussion, particularly as renewed hostilities continue to affect American forces stationed across the Middle East. Military analysts note that casualty reporting plays an important role in maintaining public transparency while documenting the human cost of military operations.

The Pentagon maintains that the revised classification is an administrative change tied to operational timelines rather than a revision of casualty records themselves. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

Operation Epic Fury began earlier in 2026 as a major U.S.-led military campaign targeting Iranian military infrastructure and strategic assets. Following weeks of combat, Washington announced a ceasefire and indicated that the operation had concluded. However, tensions quickly escalated again after attacks on commercial shipping and American military positions led to renewed military exchanges between the United States and Iran. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

According to Pentagon officials, the new casualty category begins with the renewed conflict that emerged after the ceasefire collapsed. American forces resumed military operations in response to Iranian attacks involving missiles, drones, and threats to maritime security in the Strait of Hormuz and surrounding areas.

Recent clashes have included attacks on U.S. military installations in Jordan and Iraq, missile interceptions by American air defence systems, and retaliatory strikes against Iran-backed armed groups operating in the region. The renewed conflict has broadened concerns about regional stability and the possibility of further escalation involving multiple Middle Eastern countries. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

Military officials argue that distinguishing casualties from separate operational phases helps accurately document the chronology of military engagements. Critics, however, contend that separating the figures may make it more difficult for the public to understand the overall human cost of the conflict.

The Pentagon continues to describe the revised reporting as a technical adjustment rather than a substantive change in casualty accounting.

Political leaders from across the United States and around the world gathered in Washington to pay tribute to the late Senator Lindsey Graham, remembering the South Carolina Republican as a 'true American original' whose influence stretched from Capitol Hill to global diplomatic circles. Memorial services at the U.S. Capitol and Washington National Cathedral drew President Donald Trump, Vice President JD Vance, members of Congress, foreign dignitaries, military officials, and longtime friends, reflecting Graham's unusually broad network of relationships developed during more than three decades in Congress. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

Speakers described Graham as a lawmaker known for his outspoken personality, willingness to work across party lines on certain issues, and deep involvement in American foreign policy. Throughout his Senate career, he became one of Washington's most recognizable advocates for a strong U.S. military, robust international alliances, and active American engagement overseas.

The services highlighted Graham's journey from a small-town upbringing in South Carolina to becoming one of the Senate's most influential voices on national security. Friends recalled his humor, persistence, and ability to build personal relationships even with political opponents.

His flag-draped casket lay in honor at the Capitol Rotunda before the funeral procession moved to Washington National Cathedral, symbolizing the respect accorded to one of the Senate's longest-serving Republican leaders. Lawmakers from both parties attended the ceremonies, emphasizing Graham's enduring presence within the institution despite frequent political disagreements.

The memorial reflected not only his domestic political influence but also his reputation abroad, where he maintained close relationships with leaders in Europe, the Middle East, and Ukraine. Those international ties became a defining element of his career and were repeatedly highlighted throughout the day's tributes. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}
